{
  "id": "Compound::DB00209 - Gene::1131",
  "verbalization": "Trospium is likely to interact with Chrm3 through a shared pathway involving G-protein coupled receptors. The evidence suggests that Trospium binds to Chrm1 or Chrm2, which are both involved in G-protein coupled acetylcholine or neurotransmitter receptor activities. These receptors are ultimately participated in by Chrm3, indicating a potential role for Trospium in modulating Chrm3 function. The overlapping involvement of Chrm1 and Chrm2 in receptor activity suggests that Trospium may influence Chrm3 through a common mechanism, possibly by affecting the activation or signaling of these receptors. The consistent binding of Trospium to Chrm1 or Chrm2, coupled with the shared participation in receptor activity by Chrm3, supports a mechanism where Trospium exerts its effects on Chrm3 via a pathway involving G-protein coupled receptors.",
